  "findings": [
    "The network is the 1.6.1 network (only `strict=True` was added to the `zip` calls), so the weights of the forward case load into both sides with identical `state_dict` keys; the seeded initialization and the dropout draws coincide as long as the training loops consume the torch RNG in the same order.",
    "The shipped gene list is trimmed: `gene_list_paccmann_network_prop` has 1955 genes in v2, not 1957, because the re-curated cell-line screens lack PRRC2B and S100A7L2. The case uses the reduced list (`gene_list_paccmann_network_prop_reduced`) on both sides, so the trim itself is not exercised here.",
    "v2 holds features as float32 and the scaler is fit on float32 values; 1.6.1 also casts the expression to float32 before its scaler, so the scaled matrices are compared at the `forward` class.",
    "The scaler is fit on the training pairs (a sample once per pair) on both sides: 1.6.1 fits it on the expression of `output.cell_line_ids`; v2 reproduces that with `landmarkGenes(fit_on_pairs=True)`.",
    "The variant bank is drawn at fit, from the sorted distinct SMILES of the training drugs, with one `default_rng(42)` stream, as in 1.6.1; the vocabulary is built from the flattened bank. A drug outside the fit gets its own SMILES in every bank slot in v2. The padding length is the featurizer option `padding_length` (1.6.1: hyperparameter `smiles_padding_length`; without it 1.6.1 used the longest variant, v2 always 512 by default) and `augment_smiles` is the option `augment`.",
    "Each batch row draws its variant with `torch.randint` on the global torch RNG, after the shuffling draw, as in 1.6.1. v2 evaluates early stopping on the plain (non-augmented) SMILES, as 1.6.1 did.",
    "RDKit versions differ between the two environments, which could change `MolToSmiles` output on rare molecules; the randomized-SMILES steps record whether it did.",
    "With batch norm, a 3-epoch fit does not reproduce the 1.6.1 test predictions to the `train` class (deviation steps `default/predictions` and `no_es/predictions`): the float32 rounding difference of the scaled expression is amplified through the bias in front of each batch norm layer, whose gradient is exactly zero (see `BATCH_NORM_EXPLANATION`). Every other parameter agrees at the `forward` class, and the variant without batch norm reproduces the predictions at the `forward` class.",
    "1.6.1 `drop_last` rule (drop a trailing batch of one only when more than one batch exists) is kept."
  ],
